sequenceDiagram
  %% Target design (agreed with Nick, 2026-11-07): each task VM host keeps one stopped base VM, `shark-base`, that never gets an identity. Its ZFS snapshot is the base a task start copies; there is no Incus image and no publish. In every Gateway release, and daily, the base VM converges and a new snapshot becomes the base, so a task start's converge stays near a no-op.
  %% status: draft
  %% command: none · in every Gateway release, and daily
  %% kind: task_vm.image_refresh
  autonumber
  %% lane GW icon=gateway pill=intent tone=blue
  %% lane SW icon=workflow pill=executes tone=green
  %% lane H icon=server pill=target tone=amber
  %% lane VM icon=server pill=base VM tone=purple
  participant GW as Gateway<br/>Laravel API
  participant SW as Swamp<br/>serve on Node 209
  participant H as Task VM host<br/>Incus over ssh
  participant VM as Base VM<br/>shark-base
  Note over GW: Admit the refresh<br/>one run per host
  %%> Started by:
  %%> - every Gateway release, from its step "Admit VM refreshes", as soon as Swamp has loaded the new commit and while the release goes on to switch: `task-vms:refresh-images --commit <sha>` runs from the new release, so the intent holds the new commit's recipe, pins and checkout, and the run uses the new commit's workflows and playbooks; a code-only release refreshes too
  %%> - the daily schedule at 03:20, and an operator
  %%> - a release asks a host again, up to 6 rounds 5 s apart, when Swamp could not take the run yet because it had just reloaded
  %%> Result:
  %%> - the candidate proof may take the shared CI reserve (slot 1), then any free task slot; task work cannot starve it, and a busy proof may still refuse it until the next trigger
  %%> - a CI-only host permits its own refresh proof, pinned to that host; Sabre refresh proves on Sabre and never spills onto another host
  %%> - one run per host, all hosts in parallel; the run never holds the release, and a host that refuses is skipped until the next trigger
  %%> - a host with a refresh open keeps it, and the newest commit waits behind it: one pending commit per host, the newest wins, and the run that ends admits it
  GW->>SW: Start run, full intent<br/>operation-task-vm-image-refresh
  SW->>H: Prepare the host<br/>shared Ansible phase
  %%> What it does:
  %%> - validates existing pool, project and bridge before changes; preserves unrelated host resources
  %%> - creates a missing native dataset pool, or an Incus-managed loop-backed ZFS pool with the configured size
  %%> - caps ARC at 2 GiB only when the actual pool source is a regular file; native-ZFS hosts keep their setting
  %%> - converges the isolated NAT bridge, ACL, project, profile, stock Ubuntu image and forwarding rule; an unchanged rerun changes nothing
  SW->>H: Log egress<br/>host bridge, log only
  %%> What it does:
  %%> - sets up the egress log of the host bridge (Ansible part `egress-log`): an nftables rule logs each new outside connection of a task VM, rsyslog writes it to its own file, and dnsmasq logs the DNS queries; nothing is blocked
  %%> - the first run sets `raw.dnsmasq`, which runs the whole setup of the bridge again and restarts its dnsmasq: running VMs lose DNS for about a second, so it should run when no provision is running; Incus then runs that dnsmasq without its AppArmor profile, as the user `incus`
  %%> - it runs before the base is touched; only a safety check fails the run (rules that do not load, a bridge with IPv6, an ACL without its drop rule) and starts no rollback; other checks warn
  SW->>H: Start the base VM<br/>incus start
  %%> What it does:
  %%> - before starting, converges the base VM's `raw.qemu.conf`: `[boot-opts]` with `menu = "on"` and `splash-time = "0"` skips OVMF's 3 s menu wait; every snapshot and task copy inherit it
  %%> - starts the stopped base VM; about 10 s to its guest agent; its containers start with it
  %%> - a host without a base VM gets it here, once: `incus init` from the stock Ubuntu VM image that host preparation copied, an empty seed volume, and a first boot that makes the `orbit` account with the key of the Swamp control Node and installs the SSH server; the converge builds everything else
  %%> - the task VM project keeps `restricted.snapshots=allow`: the base is a snapshot, and only a host API client can take one
  %%> - `task-vms:refresh-image <host> --from-stock` withdraws the recorded base at admission, so no task start copies it, and the run first deletes the base VM and its seed with their snapshots and builds the base from stock; after a failure the next refresh builds it as on a new host
  SW->>VM: Converge<br/>the same converge as a task start
  %%> What it does:
  %%> - each part runs only when its digest changed: the outer VM, the warm checkout at the release commit, each container
  %%> - the refresh owns the containers' `base` snapshots and retakes them when the fleet changed; the nested Gateway's services restart only when `apps/gateway` changed, and its agent delivery manifest is placed only when the pinned agent build changed
  %%> - a base VM without a nested fleet builds it here, once, in the fleet phase: three system containers from stock Ubuntu on the nested network, the nested Gateway prepared by one local `gateway-bootstrap` run, its own Node, `gateway` and `websocket` converges, app-dev and app-prod added through its own Orbit, the checkout as their disk device, a Doctor wait, then the operator join
  %%> - the build runs only when the fleet is not complete: a container is missing, or the operator stamp that the join writes last is not recorded; it removes that stamp and any container first and builds whole. A complete fleet skips it and restarts no container
  %%> - no other wait for the nested Gateway and no identity wipe: the base VM never gets an identity, and readiness belongs to a task start
  %%> - the boot phase bakes the Orbit root CA and the firewall into the base, and the `orbit-identity` unit that mounts a copy's identity share and applies it before sshd, WireGuard and the agent; a base VM has no share, so the unit skips
  %%> - the boot phase also matches the uplink by type and name (`00-orbit-task-vm.network`), never by the base VM's MAC, so every copy gets an address; it waits online for IPv4 only, masks the apt and motd timers, and starts Docker at boot, as its package does
  SW->>H: Stop and snapshot<br/>VM and seed
  %%> What it does:
  %%> - stops the base VM and snapshots it and its seed volume under the run's name; ZFS snapshots, about 1 s
  SW->>H: Prove the candidate<br/>one task start
  %%> What it does:
  %%> - steps 2 to 4 of a task start from the new snapshot in a free slot, then its release
  SW->>H: Prune old snapshots<br/>keep -previous
  %%> What it does:
  %%> - keeps the new snapshot and the one before it; removes older ones of the VM and the seed
  SW->>H: Remove host leftovers<br/>after the prune
  %%> What it does:
  %%> - in the task VM project: every image but the stock source image, the volumes of retired image seeds (`orbit-topology-seed-*`) and the slot-pool baseline (`orbit-baseline-*`)
  %%> - the retired projects the Gateway names (`orbit-baselines`, `orbit-measure-nr`), with what they hold, and the old images of `orbit-proto`
  %%> - only where the project keeps its own images and volumes; a project that cannot be emptied is reported after the others had their turn
  %%> - a failure here fails no refresh: the next refresh removes what is left, and the Operation's result says `host_clean: failed`
  alt the run passes
    SW-->>GW: Run succeeded<br/>new snapshot
    Note over GW: Promote<br/>record the new snapshot
    %%> Result:
    %%> - task starts copy the new snapshot; the one before it is `-previous`
    %%> - target: about 25 s for a no-op refresh before the proof (boot 10 s, converge 8 s, stop 5 s, snapshot 1 s); the first refresh of a new host builds the whole base, so it takes much longer: about 30 min of it is the fleet build
  else a step failed
    SW--xGW: Run failed<br/>failed step
    %%> On failure:
    %%> - the base VM is stopped and rolled back to the recorded snapshot, and a new snapshot is removed; task starts keep the recorded one
  end
